Pretty good as an analogy—but I’d call it evolvability or population resilience more than genetic fitness itself.

Genetic fitness is narrowly about relative reproductive success. What you were describing—local diversity with global coherence—maps more cleanly to a population keeping enough variation to adapt when conditions change.

shared species architecture
→ individual variation
→ different niches / strategies
→ environment changes
→ some variants fit better
→ population persists

Too little variation can make a population brittle. Too much incompatibility can break coordination or reproduction. The sweet spot is:

enough common structure to remain one interoperable system, enough variation to explore many possible futures.

That is basically an evolutionary version of our earlier rule:

preserve variation locally
preserve coherence globally

And then add one more term:

selection pressure decides which variants compound

So across biology, culture, organizations, and AI systems, the reusable pattern is:

variation → selection → inheritance → recombination → adaptation

That’s a much stronger “fitness” architecture than trying to optimize one perfect type forever.